   101	## 6. Crypto / Keychain Commands
   102	
   103	Current source:
   104	- `apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/commands/crypto.rs`
   105	- `apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/commands/keychain.rs`
   106	- `apps/desktop/src-tauri/capabilities/plugin-account-crypto.json`
   107	- `apps/desktop/src-tauri/capabilities/plugin-account-keychain.json`
   108	
   109	| Command | Allowed windows | Security rule |
   110	|---|---|---|
   111	| `crypto_encrypt_for` | `account`,`control` | plaintext in,encrypted envelope out; key resolved in Rust |
   112	| `crypto_wrap_dek_for_devices` | `account`,`control` | DEK handle only |
   113	| `crypto_unwrap_dek_for_device` | `account`,`control` | returns handle,not raw key |
   114	| `crypto_recovery_sign` | `account`,`control` | transcript signed in Rust |
   115	| `secret_set` / `secret_get` / `secret_del` | `account`,`control` | Keychain scoped by account/device. Runtime `commands::keychain::KEYCHAIN_ALLOWED_WINDOWS` (`account`, `control`) check rejects any other origin (widget / pet / ai-cube / grid / main / console) with `E3004` before reaching the platform layer. |
   116	
   117	Raw DEK must never cross IPC. JS may hold opaque handle ids only.
   118	
   119	### 6.0.1 Keychain ↔ KeyVault opaque-handle boundary (G2.4)
   120	
   121	JS-visible Keychain surface is limited to the three `secret_*` commands;
   122	JS-visible KeyVault surface is limited to `KeyHandleId` integers returned
   123	from `crypto_*` commands. The ONE authorised crossing between the two —
   124	"Keychain bytes → KeyVault handle" — lives in
   125	`apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/crypto/keychain_handle.rs`:
   126	
   127	| Helper | Direction | Behaviour |
   128	|---|---|---|
   129	| `keychain_handle::load_kek_into_vault(key, vault)` | Keychain → KeyVault | `secret_get` then `KeyVault::insert_kek`. Zeroizes the byte buffer before returning. Result is only `KeyHandleId`. |
   130	| `keychain_handle::insert_kek_from_bytes(bytes, vault)` | caller bytes → KeyVault | Same insertion + zeroize for callers that already hold bytes (e.g. recovery flow). |
   131	
   132	Rule (machine-enforced by code review + the dedicated tests):
   133	
   134	- `secret_get` MUST NOT be called by any Tauri command that exposes its
   135	  return value to JS for KEK / DEK / device-private bytes. Such material
   136	  must flow through `keychain_handle::*` so JS only ever sees a handle.
   137	- `secret_get` is allowed to surface bytes to JS for **non-key material**:
   138	  refresh tokens, account-id markers, encrypted recovery transcripts, etc.
   139	
   140	Rust-internal errors (do not cross IPC; calling Tauri commands that use
   141	this bridge must map them to JS-visible `E11xx` / `E13xx` variants):
   142	
